# [DD-13b.W23.3a] §1.10's WHOLE MECHANISM IN ONE CELL: a flat splice, one
# fragment, no nesting.
#
# `include_basic_frag.rxtfrag` is NOT independently discoverable — the
# `.rxtfrag` extension is load-bearing, `tests/rxtsource/CLAUDE.md`'s own
# rule: a fragment copied in as `.rxt` would become its own discoverable
# entry and this fixture would test the opposite of its own subject — but
# IS present in the same scratch directory this entry resolves its
# `include` line against, so the resolution succeeds.
#
# W23-S7 asserts legs A and B agree on the closure's shape (§1.10.2's
# rules 2 and 4): leg A's `--list-source` reports the resolved `include`
# row (`name` = the real path, `value` = the path as written); leg B's
# `run.sh` reports `entry files: 1` / `fragments spliced: 1`, and the
# entry's own case count EXCEEDS its own file's case count by the
# fragment's own — K35's own lesson, a splice check satisfied by a
# closure of zero would prove nothing.
#
# LEG C DOES NOT PARTICIPATE, and this is a RULING (w233a_report.md §2,
# accepted by the manager): `include` is head-scoped by design (§2.5),
# so this file is head-bearing, and `verify_rxt.py`'s seam-ruling
# refusal — UNCHANGED — already raises on it before any body line is
# reached, exactly as it does for `dup_head_description.rxtin` one
# production over. Three legs cannot compare a block count leg C is
# structurally refused from ever seeing.
include "include_basic_frag.rxtfrag"

pattern top
m "top" 0 3
